the most important utilities that live under that specific toolset umbrella:

oleid (The Triager)
Purpose: Quickly scans an Office file to see if it looks suspicious.
What it extracts: It outputs a handy ASCII table showing whether the file contains VBA macros, embedded Flash objects,
or file encryption.

olevba (The Macro Extractor)
Purpose: One of the most famous tools in the suite. It extracts and analyzes VBA Macro source code from MS Office files 
in clear text.
What it extracts: It flags dangerous keywords (like AutoOpen, Shell, or ShellExecute), automatically decodes obfuscated 
strings (Hex, Base64), and grabs IOCs like malicious IP addresses or URLs.

mraptor / MacroRaptor (The Rule Engine)
Purpose: Uses a lightweight heuristic rule system to instantly determine if a VBA macro is malicious or safe.

oleobj & rtfobj (The Object Extractors)
Purpose: Drops and extracts embedded objects (like hidden executables, scripts, or images) bound inside standard OLE or
RTF documents.

msodde (The Dynamic Data Exchange Finder)
Purpose: Detects and extracts DDE/DDEAUTO links from Office documents. Hackers often use DDE to execute command-line 
payloads directly through Word without needing macros enabled.

olemeta & oletimes (The Timestampers)
Purpose: Pulls metadata properties, author info, creation time, and modification timestamps from a document. This is 
essential for timeline tracking during cyber forensic investigations.
